ASSISTANT # What `llm-providers/prelude.nix` settles
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT **Short version: it doesn't change the viability verdict — the provider types are orthogonal to repo exposure.** But it does confirm two things worth noting.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## The design boundary is clean
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The provider types (`provider-type`, `user-system-type`, etc.) cover *connectivity*: endpoint, credentials, model name. Nothing in them touches the repo/workspace concern, and nothing should — the repo share is agent-side machinery. Concretely, if the repo-filesystem feature needs any configuration at all (e.g. whether to expose `.git`, where the egress dir lives), those options belong as siblings of `provider` in the `spec` submodule in `llm-agent/config.nix`, not here.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT In fact, given the option description already frames the tool as running agents "within the context of the repository", repo exposure is arguably the default behavior — you may need **no new options at all**, just the unconditional share. I'd start there and only add options when a real use case demands it.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## The host→guest pattern is confirmed consistent
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT The key path (`key-script` → launcher writes `$DEVTOOLS_CONFIG/users/<name>.key` → read-only virtfs → guest mount) and the proposed repo path (`DEVTOOLS_REPO` → read-only virtfs → guest mount → overlay) are the same shape. The prelude types confirm there's no hidden coupling that the new share would disturb — `setup.nix` consumes `provider.*` purely as config values.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## One tangential observation
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT `provider-type.models` defines only `main`, but its description promises "The models that will be available as workers are specified in this section as well" — there's no `workers` option. A doc/type mismatch, unrelated to this task, but worth a ticket at some point. (If workers do arrive later, each might want its own overlay upper dir — a reason to keep the workspace layout per-agent rather than global.)
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ## Files
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT Nothing further is needed for the viability call — the analysis is fully grounded now. The only remaining optional addition is `journal/feature/agentic-dev/session-contexts/langchain sandbox/session.md`, and only if you want help with implementation details or drafting the task notes rather than the viability assessment itself.
